  "fact_notes": [
    "オリジナル教材。一般的原理と架空の例を用いた文章。架空例は実在の調査・実証結果ではない。制作台帳は来歴確認用。"
  ],
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "Four students are preparing a presentation about their town.",
          "ja": "四人の生徒が自分の町についての発表を準備している。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Three members frequently exchange ideas, while the fourth student, Mina, rarely speaks during their meetings.",
          "ja": "三人は頻繁に考えを交換するが四人目のミナは会合中めったに話さない。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The other students initially assume that she is uninterested in the project.",
          "ja": "他生徒は当初彼女が活動に無関心と想定する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "They therefore begin dividing the remaining tasks without asking for her opinion.",
          "ja": "そこで彼女の意見を尋ねず残りの作業を分け始める。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "One afternoon, a teacher asks the group to explain how each member has contributed.",
          "ja": "ある午後、教師は各班員の貢献を説明するよう班に頼む。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Mina shows the others a notebook containing several detailed suggestions for improving their presentation.",
          "ja": "ミナは発表改善の詳細な提案が複数入ったノートを見せる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "She explains that the discussion often moves to another topic before she has finished organizing her thoughts.",
          "ja": "考えをまとめ終える前に議論が別話題へ移ることが多いと説明する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The group realizes that silence does not necessarily indicate a lack of ideas or interest.",
          "ja": "沈黙は必ずしも考えや関心の不足を示さないと班は気づく。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "They decide to try a different arrangement at their next meeting.",
          "ja": "次の会合で別のやり方を試すと決める。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Each student first writes one or two suggestions independently before the group begins discussing them.",
          "ja": "班が議論を始める前に各生徒がまず一、二の提案を独力で書く。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Members then take turns explaining the reasons behind their suggestions.",
          "ja": "次に順番に提案の理由を説明する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "They can still respond freely to one another once everyone has had an opportunity to contribute.",
          "ja": "全員が貢献する機会を得た後も自由に互いに応答できる。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "Mina finds it easier to offer her ideas under the new arrangement.",
          "ja": "ミナは新しいやり方で考えを出しやすいと感じる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "The other students also discover that writing before speaking helps them notice weaknesses in some of their own proposals.",
          "ja": "他生徒も話す前に書くことが自身の提案の弱点に気づく助けになると発見する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "However, they do not conclude that every conversation requires exactly the same procedure.",
          "ja": "しかし全会話に全く同じ手順が必要とは結論づけない。"
        },
        {
          "en": "They agree to choose their discussion method according to the purpose of a particular meeting.",
          "ja": "特定会合の目的に応じ議論方法を選ぶことに合意する。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p5",
      "sentences": [
        {
          "en": "A group's success depends partly on how its members are able to share what they know.",
          "ja": "班の成功はメンバーが知識をどう共有できるかにも依存する。"
        },
        {
          "en": "A person who speaks less frequently may still have something valuable to say.",
          "ja": "話す頻度が低い人にも価値ある発言があるかもしれない。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Creating different opportunities to contribute can help a group discover ideas that would otherwise remain unnoticed.",
          "ja": "異なる貢献機会を作るとそうでなければ見逃される考えを発見する助けになる。"
        },
        {
          "en": "Good cooperation involves listening as well as speaking.",
          "ja": "良い協力は話すだけでなく聞くことも伴う。"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
